// @expect-error Class 'Child' declares no constructor, so it would run the zero-argument constructor of its base class 'Base', which declares none. Mark 'constructor(int)' in 'Base' as 'protected' so a subclass can call it, then declare a constructor that calls 'super(...)', as in 'constructor(int n) { super(n); }'.
// @expect-error Class 'OverSized' declares no constructor, so it would run the zero-argument constructor of its base class 'Sized', which declares none. Declare a constructor that calls 'super(...)', as in 'constructor(int width) { super(width); }'.
// @expect-error Constructor of 'Unchained' does not call 'super(...)', so it would run the zero-argument constructor of its base class 'Sized', which declares none. Start this constructor's body with 'super(width);' and add 'int width' to its parameters.
// @expect-error Class 'OverGuarded' declares no constructor, so it would run the zero-argument constructor of its base class 'Guarded', which is private to 'Guarded'. Mark 'constructor()' in 'Guarded' as 'protected' so a subclass can call it.
// @expect-error Constructor of 'UnderGuarded' does not call 'super(...)', so it would run the zero-argument constructor of its base class 'Guarded', which is private to 'Guarded'. Mark 'constructor()' in 'Guarded' as 'protected' so a subclass can call it.
// @expect-error The constructor of 'Base' is private, so only code inside 'Base' can call it. Mark it 'protected' to call it from a subclass.
// @expect-error Class 'Failure' declares no constructor, so it would run the zero-argument constructor of its base class 'Error', which declares none. Declare a constructor that calls 'super(...)', as in 'constructor(string message) { super(message); }'.
// @expect-error Constructor of 'Bottom' cannot initialize base class 'Middle<Mute>', which declares no constructor and so runs the zero-argument constructor of 'Top<Mute>': the zero-argument constructor of 'Top<T>' exists only when 'T' satisfies 'Speaker', and in 'Top<Mute>', 'T' is 'Mute', which does not implement 'Speaker'. Give 'Top' a zero-argument constructor that exists for every type argument, or use a type argument that satisfies the bound.
// @expect-error Constructor of 'SuperBottom' cannot initialize base class 'Middle<Mute>', which declares no constructor and so runs the zero-argument constructor of 'Top<Mute>': the zero-argument constructor of 'Top<T>' exists only when 'T' satisfies 'Speaker', and in 'Top<Mute>', 'T' is 'Mute', which does not implement 'Speaker'. Give 'Top' a zero-argument constructor that exists for every type argument, or use a type argument that satisfies the bound.
// @expect-error Cannot construct 'Bare': it declares no constructor, and neither does its base class 'Middle<Mute>', so it needs the zero-argument constructor of 'Top<Mute>', but the zero-argument constructor of 'Top<T>' exists only when 'T' satisfies 'Speaker', and in 'Top<Mute>', 'T' is 'Mute', which does not implement 'Speaker'. Give 'Top' a zero-argument constructor that exists for every type argument, or use a type argument that satisfies the bound.
// @expect-error build failed with 10 problems
// A class that declares no constructor runs the zero-argument constructor of its base class. A
// base that declares constructors must have one the class can reach. The refusal is reported
// once, at the class. Building the class with 'new' or calling 'super()' on it reports nothing
// further. A declared constructor without 'super(...)' is held to the same rule. An unmarked
// constructor of a public class is private to it, so a subclass cannot call it. The chain
// passes over a base class with no constructor. A constructor below it that reaches an excluded
// conditional constructor further up is refused, with or without 'super()', and so is building a
// class below it that declares none.
public class Base {
    protected int n;

    constructor(int n) {
        this.n = n;
    }
}

class Child extends Base {
}

class Sized {
    protected int width;

    protected constructor(int width, string label = "wide") {
        this.width = width;
    }
}

class OverSized extends Sized {
}

class Further extends OverSized {
    protected constructor() {
        super();
    }
}

class Unchained extends Sized {
    protected constructor() { }
}

public class Guarded {
    constructor() { }
}

class OverGuarded extends Guarded {
}

class UnderGuarded extends Guarded {
    protected constructor() { }
}

class CallsBase extends Base {
    protected constructor() {
        super(1);
    }
}

class Failure extends Error {
}

interface Speaker {
    speak() -> string;
}

class Mute {
    protected constructor() { }
}

class Top<T> {
    protected constructor<T: Speaker>() { }

    protected constructor(int size) { }
}

class Middle<T> extends Top<T> {
}

class Bottom extends Middle<Mute> {
    protected constructor() { }
}

class Bare extends Middle<Mute> {
}

class SuperBottom extends Middle<Mute> {
    protected constructor() {
        super();
    }
}

main() -> int {
    let child = new Child();
    let further = new Further();
    let bottom = new Bottom();
    let superBottom = new SuperBottom();
    let bare = new Bare();
    return child.n;
}
